arXiv:2602.20590v1 [eess.SP] 24 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Provable orbit recovery over <math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> from the non-uniform second moment</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tamir Bendory
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dan Edidin
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Josh Katz<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup"><math id="m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math></sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shay Kreymer
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nir Sharon
</span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 24, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the recovery of an unknown three-dimensional band-limited signal from multiple noisy observations that are randomly rotated by latent elements of <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the rotations are drawn from an unknown, non-uniform distribution. Because the rotations are unobserved, only the signal orbit under the rotation group can be recovered. We show that the signal orbit and the rotation distribution are jointly identifiable from the first and second moments. This yields an improved high-noise sample complexity that scales quadratically with the noise variance, rather than cubically as in the uniform-rotation case. We further develop a provable, computationally efficient reconstruction algorithm that recovers the 3-D signal by successively solving a sequence of well-conditioned linear systems. The algorithm is validated through extensive numerical experiments. Our results provide a principled and tractable framework for high-noise 3-D orbit recovery, with potential relevance to cryo-electron microscopy and cryo-electron tomography modeling, where molecules are observed in unknown orientations.</p>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">$\ast$</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">$\ast$</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Approved for Public Release; Distribution Unlimited. Public Release Case Number 26-0162. Affiliation with the MITRE Corporation is for identification purposes only and is not intended to convey or imply MITRE’s concurrence with, or support for, the positions, opinions, or viewpoints expressed by the
author. ©2026 The MITRE Corporation. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the problem of recovering a 3‑D signal from its randomly rotated and noisy observations. Specifically, we aim to estimate an unknown signal <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> samples of the form</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{i}=g_{i}\cdot x+\varepsilon_{i},\quad i=1,\ldots,n," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=g_{i}\cdot x+\varepsilon_{i},\quad i=1,\ldots,n,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are random elements drawn from a distribution <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> on the rotation group <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. Gaussian noise terms with zero mean and variance <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite-dimensional space of band‑limited functions on <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is a non‑uniform distribution over <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>; precise mathematical models are provided in the following sections. Our goal is to recover <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> from the observations <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},\ldots,y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},\ldots,y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the rotations <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\ldots,g_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\ldots,g_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> being unknown and the noise level <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> potentially large. Importantly, since both the signal and the rotations are unknown, <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is identifiable only up to a global rotation; that is, only its <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit can be recovered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The model (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is a special case of a broader class of problems in which the goal is to estimate a signal from noisy samples subject to unknown group actions. This class is commonly known as <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">orbit recovery</em> problems or <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multi-reference alignment</em>, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. When the noise level is low, a standard approach is to first estimate the latent group elements from the samples, then align the observations accordingly, and finally average to reduce noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. In high-noise regimes, however, reliably estimating these group elements (e.g., rotations) becomes difficult, making alignment-based procedures unreliable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. In such settings, one typically turns to methods that estimate the signal directly while marginalizing over the unknown transformations; two popular methods are the expectation-maximization algorithm and the method of moments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. This work focuses on the latter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivation: Electron-microscopy-based technologies for structural biology.</span>
The primary motivation for studying orbit recovery problems of the form (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) comes from single-particle cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM), the leading technology for determining the 3-D structure of biological macromolecules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. In cryo-EM, one seeks to recover a 3-D (band-limited) function representing the molecular density from many noisy 2-D tomographic projections. Each projection corresponds to the molecule viewed after an unknown, randomly drawn 3-D rotation, so the observations are related through latent group actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Model (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) captures the same central statistical difficulty—estimating a signal from multiple noisy, randomly rotated copies—while abstracting away the additional complication introduced by the projection operator. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5. The path to provable algorithms for cryo-EM? ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we make this connection precise by relating (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to the cryo-EM forward model and outlining how the methods developed here can be potentially extended to incorporate projections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Model (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is also closely connected to cryo-electron tomography (cryo-ET), an emerging imaging modality for visualizing cellular architecture, including organelles and macromolecular assemblies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. In cryo-ET, a 3-D specimen (e.g., a cell) is imaged by collecting a tilt series of 2-D projections at known viewing angles. These projections are then integrated to produce a 3-D tomogram that preserves the <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in situ</span> spatial arrangement of macromolecules within the cell. Researchers often extract smaller 3-D sub-volumes from the tomogram, called subtomograms, centered on macromolecules of interest.
While real tomograms contain a heterogeneous mixture of structures, it is useful to consider an idealized setting in which the volume contains many instances of a single underlying structure <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, each appearing in an unknown, random orientation. Under this abstraction, the statistical structure aligns naturally with (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), which models observations as noisy, randomly transformed copies of a common signal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The method of moments and sample complexity.</span>
A remarkable line of work has revealed a close connection between the method of moments and the information-theoretic limits of orbit recovery. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, it was shown that, in the high-noise regime, the minimal number of samples required for accurate signal estimation (i.e., the sample complexity) scales as <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the smallest moment order that uniquely determines the signal’s orbit from the observations.
For instance, in model (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> transformations drawn uniformly at random, one has <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>, and consequently the sample complexity scales as <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. More generally, the <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> scaling also arises in many orbit recovery models under uniform group actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contribution.</span>
Much of the existing work on orbit recovery problems focuses on identifiability: determining when a moment-matching formulation, which typically involves a system of polynomial equations, admits a unique solution and its implication on sample complexity. In practice, however, the field remains largely driven by heuristic algorithms that lack rigorous theoretical guarantees; prominent examples include <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.
This paper makes a significant step forward by introducing a computationally efficient algorithm for signal recovery from the second moment when the distribution of rotations is non-uniform, accompanied by provable theoretical guarantees.
We show that, in this setting, the unknown signal and the rotation distribution are identifiable from the second moment, which implies a sample complexity scaling as <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. We further develop a computationally efficient, provable, and stable recovery algorithm that reconstructs the 3-D function by successively solving a sequence of well-conditioned linear systems. This result complements our previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, which addressed recovery under uniformly distributed rotations via the third moment, and generalized <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> from <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(2)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Paper organization.</span>
The paper is organized as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reviews relevant harmonic analysis on compact groups and formulates the moments in representation-theoretic terms. Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents and proves the main results on signal recovery from the second moment. Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> introduces the recovery algorithm—based on successively solving linear systems—and demonstrates its effectiveness numerically, including empirical studies of robustness to noise and the impact of the distribution. Finally, Section <a href="#S5" title="5. The path to provable algorithms for cryo-EM? ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> outlines potential extensions of the approach to the cryo-EM problem.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Harmonic analysis on compact groups</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in L^{2}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in L^{2}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by its <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fourier coefficients</span> for each irreducible representation <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of G. If <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon G\to U(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon G\to U(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is a unitary representation of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the Fourier coefficient of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is the operator on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(V)=\int_{G}\rho(g)\varphi_{V}(g)^{*}\;dg," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(V)=\int_{G}\rho(g)\varphi_{V}(g)^{*}\;dg,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dg</annotation></semantics></math> is the Haar measure on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with total integral one, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{V}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{V}(g)</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{V}(g_{i})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{V}(g_{i})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the adjoint.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of distinct irreducible representations of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is recovered from its Fourier coefficients by the Fourier inversion formula</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(g)=\sum_{V\in\hat{G}}(\dim V)\Tr(\hat{\rho}(V)\varphi_{V}(g))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(g)=\sum_{V\in\hat{G}}(\dim V)\Tr(\hat{\rho}(V)\varphi_{V}(g)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the case where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(g)dg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(g)dg</annotation></semantics></math> defines a probability distribution, then the normalization condition <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{G}\rho(g)\;dg=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{G}\rho(g)\;dg=1</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(\mathbf{1})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(\mathbf{1})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟏</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the trivial representation. The uniform distribution corresponds to the case
where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-operator for all non-trivial irreducible representations.
By contrast, for a “generic” distribution, the Fourier coefficients <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> are invertible
operators for all irreducibles <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.1. </span>Harmonic analysis over <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given our focus on the group <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>, we briefly review the spherical harmonics and Wigner <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices, which provide explicit bases for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\SO(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\SO(3))</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">spherical harmonics</span> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\ell}^{m}:S^{2}\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\ell}^{m}:S^{2}\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\ell\leq m\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\ell\leq m\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math> form an orthonormal basis for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the Condon–Shortley phase convention, they are defined as</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{\ell}^{m}(\theta,\varphi)=\sqrt{\frac{2\ell+1}{4\pi}\frac{(\ell-m)!}{(\ell+m)!}}P_{\ell}^{m}(\cos\theta)e^{im\varphi}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{\ell}^{m}(\theta,\varphi)=\sqrt{\frac{2\ell+1}{4\pi}\frac{(\ell-m)!}{(\ell+m)!}}P_{\ell}^{m}(\cos\theta)e^{im\varphi},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the associated Legendre polynomial. For each <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, the span</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\ell}=\mathrm{span}\{Y_{\ell}^{m}:-\ell\leq m\leq\ell\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\ell}=\mathrm{span}\{Y_{\ell}^{m}:-\ell\leq m\leq\ell\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">is an irreducible representation of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>. The Peter-Weyl decomposition gives</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(S^{2})=\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{\infty}H_{\ell}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(S^{2})=\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{\infty}H_{\ell}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">A signal in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-band limited if it lies in the subspace <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\oplus_{\ell=0}^{L}H_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus_{\ell=0}^{L}H_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. Throughout the paper, we consider band-limited signals and use the notation
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(S^{2})_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(S^{2})_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the subspace of
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-band-limited signals in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, the representation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\ell}:\SO(3)\to U(2\ell+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\ell}:\SO(3)\to U(2\ell+1)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> by rotation of functions. The matrix elements of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> in the spherical harmonic basis are the <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wigner <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-functions</span>:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)=\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},g\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)=\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},g\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using the <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">ZYZ</span> Euler angle parameterization <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=R_{z}(\alpha)R_{y}(\beta)R_{z}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=R_{z}(\alpha)R_{y}(\beta)R_{z}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\gamma\in[0,2\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\gamma\in[0,2\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0,\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0,\pi]</annotation></semantics></math>, the Wigner <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-functions factor as</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)=e^{-im\alpha}d_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(\beta)e^{-im^{\prime}\gamma}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)=e^{-im\alpha}d_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(\beta)e^{-im^{\prime}\gamma},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Wigner small-<math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> matrix, which can be expressed in terms of Jacobi polynomials.
The normalized functions <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2\ell+1}\,D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2\ell+1}\,D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> form an orthonormal basis for <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\SO(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\SO(3))</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the Haar measure. Consequently, any probability density <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))</annotation></semantics></math> admits the Fourier expansion</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(g)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}(2\ell+1)\operatorname{Tr}\left(\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})D^{\ell}(g)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(g)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}(2\ell+1)\operatorname{Tr}\left(\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})D^{\ell}(g)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where the Fourier coefficient <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})\in\mathbb{C}^{(2\ell+1)\times(2\ell+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})\in\mathbb{C}^{(2\ell+1)\times(2\ell+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})_{m,m^{\prime}}=\int_{\SO(3)}\rho(g)\overline{D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)}\,dg." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})_{m,m^{\prime}}=\int_{\SO(3)}\rho(g)\overline{D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)}\,dg.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Throughout the text, we use the notations <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})_{m,m^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})_{m,m^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> interchangeably to represent the coefficients that represent the distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.2. </span>Clebsch-Gordan coefficients</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The tensor product of two irreducible representations decomposes as</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\ell_{1}}\otimes H_{\ell_{2}}\cong\bigoplus_{k=|\ell_{1}-\ell_{2}|}^{\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}}H_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\ell_{1}}\otimes H_{\ell_{2}}\cong\bigoplus_{k=|\ell_{1}-\ell_{2}|}^{\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}}H_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Clebsch-Gordan (CG) coefficients</span> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\ell_{1}m_{1}\,\ell_{2}m_{2}|\ell_{3}m_{3}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\ell_{1}m_{1}\,\ell_{2}m_{2}|\ell_{3}m_{3}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> provide the explicit intertwining maps. They satisfy the selection rule <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}+m_{2}=m_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}+m_{2}=m_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and vanish unless <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\ell_{1}-\ell_{2}|\leq\ell_{3}\leq\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\ell_{1}-\ell_{2}|\leq\ell_{3}\leq\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. These coefficients are central to our second moment formulas, as they govern the projection <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\ell_{2}}\otimes H_{\ell_{3}}\to H_{\ell_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\ell_{2}}\otimes H_{\ell_{3}}\to H_{\ell_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and they are explicitly given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle\ell_{1}\,m_{1}\,\ell_{2}\,m_{2}|\ell\,m\rangle=\delta_{m,m_{1}+m_{2}}\sqrt{\frac{(2\ell+1)(\ell+\ell_{1}-\ell_{2})!(\ell-\ell_{1}+\ell_{2})!(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}-\ell)!}{(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}+\ell+1)!}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle\ell_{1}\,m_{1}\,\ell_{2}\,m_{2}|\ell\,m\rangle=\delta_{m,m_{1}+m_{2}}\sqrt{\frac{(2\ell+1)(\ell+\ell_{1}-\ell_{2})!(\ell-\ell_{1}+\ell_{2})!(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}-\ell)!}{(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}+\ell+1)!}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;\times\sqrt{(\ell+m)!(\ell-m)!(\ell_{1}-m_{1})!(\ell_{1}+m_{1})!(\ell_{2}-m_{2})!(\ell_{2}+m_{2})!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;\times\sqrt{(\ell+m)!(\ell-m)!(\ell_{1}-m_{1})!(\ell_{1}+m_{1})!(\ell_{2}-m_{2})!(\ell_{2}+m_{2})!}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\;\times\sum_{k}\frac{(-1)^{k}}{k!(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}-\ell-k)!(\ell_{1}-m_{1}-k)!(\ell_{2}+m_{2}-k)!(\ell-\ell_{2}+m_{1}+k)!(\ell-\ell_{1}-m_{2}+k)!}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;\times\sum_{k}\frac{(-1)^{k}}{k!(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2}-\ell-k)!(\ell_{1}-m_{1}-k)!(\ell_{2}+m_{2}-k)!(\ell-\ell_{2}+m_{1}+k)!(\ell-\ell_{1}-m_{2}+k)!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The following lemma gives the explicit relationship between the projection operators and the Clebsch-Gordan coefficients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a given <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\ell}=(a^{\ell}_{-\ell},\ldots,a^{\ell}_{\ell})\in H_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\ell}=(a^{\ell}_{-\ell},\ldots,a^{\ell}_{\ell})\in H_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a vector
expanded in the spherical harmonic basis. For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\ell_{2}}-{\ell_{3}}|\leq\ell_{1}\leq\ell_{2}+\ell_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\ell_{2}}-{\ell_{3}}|\leq\ell_{1}\leq\ell_{2}+\ell_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the projection <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a^{\ell_{2}}\otimes a^{\ell_{3}})_{\ell_{1}}\in H_{\ell_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a^{\ell_{2}}\otimes a^{\ell_{3}})_{\ell_{1}}\in H_{\ell_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> has components with respect to the
spherical harmonic basis of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\ell_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\ell_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl[(a^{\ell_{2}}\otimes a^{\ell_{3}})_{\ell_{1}}\bigr]_{k_{1}}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}k_{2},k_{3}\\
k_{2}+k_{3}=k_{1}\end{subarray}}\langle\ell_{2}\,k_{2}\;\ell_{3}\,k_{3}\mid\ell_{1}\,k_{1}\rangle\,a^{\ell_{2}}_{k_{2}}\,a^{\ell_{3}}_{k_{3}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl[(a^{\ell_{2}}\otimes a^{\ell_{3}})_{\ell_{1}}\bigr]_{k_{1}}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}k_{2},k_{3}\\
k_{2}+k_{3}=k_{1}\end{subarray}}\langle\ell_{2}\,k_{2}\;\ell_{3}\,k_{3}\mid\ell_{1}\,k_{1}\rangle\,a^{\ell_{2}}_{k_{2}}\,a^{\ell_{3}}_{k_{3}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\ell_{1}\leq k_{1}\leq\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\ell_{1}\leq k_{1}\leq\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the sum is over <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|k_{2}|\leq\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|k_{2}|\leq\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|k_{3}|\leq\ell_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|k_{3}|\leq\ell_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.3. </span>In-plane uniform distributions</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we introduce and study in-plane-uniform distributions. As will be explained in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, these distributions play a key role in cryo-EM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A probability distribution <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))</annotation></semantics></math> is in-plane uniform if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(gh)=\rho(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(gh)=\rho(g)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\SO(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\SO(2)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(2)</annotation></semantics></math> is embedded as rotations about the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-axis; i.e., matrices of the form</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta&amp;\sin\theta&amp;0\\
-\sin\theta&amp;\cos\theta&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;1\end{pmatrix},\quad\theta\in[0,2\pi)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta&amp;\sin\theta&amp;0\\
-\sin\theta&amp;\cos\theta&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;1\end{pmatrix},\quad\theta\in[0,2\pi).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Such distributions correspond to viewing directions that are uniformly distributed in the in-plane angle but may have an arbitrary tilt distribution.
Note that if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is in-plane uniform, then <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\cdot\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\cdot\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is as well, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\cdot\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\cdot\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))</annotation></semantics></math>
is the function defined by the formula <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g\cdot\rho)(g^{\prime})=\rho(g^{-1}g^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g\cdot\rho)(g^{\prime})=\rho(g^{-1}g^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\in G</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, an in-plane uniform distribution is a function <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\SO(3))^{\SO(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\SO(3))^{\SO(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(2)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h\rho)(g)=\rho(gh)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h\rho)(g)=\rho(gh)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since this action commutes with the usual
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> action on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\SO(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\SO(3))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\SO(3))^{\SO(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\SO(3))^{\SO(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> sub-representation. The following lemma shows that, viewed as a function on <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>, elements
of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\SO(3))^{\SO(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\SO(3))^{\SO(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> can be characterized in terms of their Fourier coefficients with respect to the bases of spherical harmonics for each irreducible representation <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With respect to the basis <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Y^{\ell}_{m}\}_{-\ell\leq m\leq\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Y^{\ell}_{m}\}_{-\ell\leq m\leq\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> of spherical
harmonics for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Fourier coefficients of a function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))^{\SO(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))^{\SO(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]=0\quad\text{unless }m^{\prime}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">unless </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]=0\quad\text{unless }m^{\prime}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">That is, only the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math> column of each Fourier coefficient matrix is non-zero.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant, we have that</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\SO(3)}\rho(g)\overline{D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)}\,dg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\SO(3)}\rho(g)\overline{D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)}\,dg</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\SO(3)}\rho(gh)\overline{D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)}\,dg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\SO(3)}\rho(gh)\overline{D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)}\,dg</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\int_{\SO(3)}\rho(g)\overline{D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(gh)}\,dg," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int_{\SO(3)}\rho(g)\overline{D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(gh)}\,dg,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the second equality follows from a change of variables <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\mapsto gh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\mapsto gh</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now,
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)=\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},g\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}}\rangle," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)=\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},g\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}}\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math>
and if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\SO(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\SO(2)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to rotation by an angle <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> about the <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-axis,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(gh)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(gh)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},(gh)\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},(gh)\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},g\cdot(h\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},g\cdot(h\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e^{\iota m^{\prime}\theta}\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},g\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e^{\iota m^{\prime}\theta}\langle Y_{\ell}^{m},g\cdot Y_{\ell}^{m^{\prime}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e^{\iota m^{\prime}\theta}D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e^{\iota m^{\prime}\theta}D_{m,m^{\prime}}^{\ell}(g),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">since the action of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(2)</annotation></semantics></math> is diagonalized with respect to the spherical harmonic basis.
Hence, we have that <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]=e^{\iota m^{\prime}\theta}\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]=e^{\iota m^{\prime}\theta}\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in[0,2\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in[0,2\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>,
so if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> these coefficients vanish.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.1.3. In-plane uniform distributions ‣ 2.1. Harmonic analysis on compact groups ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> establishes that the space of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(2)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant
functions is isomorphic to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(S^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(S^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
via the isomorphism <math id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)/\SO(2)\cong S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)/\SO(2)\cong S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Signal and distribution models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model the target signal <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> as a real-valued, radially discretized band-limited function supported at <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> spherical shells within the unit ball in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Expanded in spherical harmonics
our function can be represented as:</p>
<table id="S2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(r,\theta,\varphi)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{L}\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell}x^{\ell}_{m}[r]\,Y_{\ell}^{m}(\theta,\varphi)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(r,\theta,\varphi)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{L}\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell}x^{\ell}_{m}[r]\,Y_{\ell}^{m}(\theta,\varphi),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1,\ldots,R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1,\ldots,R</annotation></semantics></math> is a discrete parameter and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta,\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta,\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> are spherical coordinates.
As such, our signal space can be identified with the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>-representation</p>
<table id="S2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{L}=\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{L}H_{\ell}^{R}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{L}=\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{L}H_{\ell}^{R}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Given a bandlimit of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on the signal and a distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in L^{2}(\mathrm{SO}(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in L^{2}(\mathrm{SO}(3))</annotation></semantics></math>,
the corresponding bandlimited distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the function obtained by expanding
the distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> up to band <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. It can be expressed in the basis of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-Wigner matrices as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{L}(g)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{L}\;\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell}\;\sum_{m^{\prime}=-\ell}^{\ell}\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]\,D^{\ell}_{m,m^{\prime}}(g),\quad g\in\SO(3)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{L}(g)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{L}\;\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell}\;\sum_{m^{\prime}=-\ell}^{\ell}\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]\,D^{\ell}_{m,m^{\prime}}(g),\quad g\in\SO(3).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>A Fourier-theoretic perspective of <math id="S2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> moments</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we consider the first two population moments</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{1}(\rho,x)\coloneqq\int_{G}\rho(g)(gx)dg," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>G</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{1}(\rho,x)\coloneqq\int_{G}\rho(g)(gx)dg,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{2}(\rho,x)\coloneq\int_{G}\rho(g)(gx)^{\otimes 2}dg." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>G</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{2}(\rho,x)\coloneq\int_{G}\rho(g)(gx)^{\otimes 2}dg.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We represent
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{L}=\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{L}H_{\ell}^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{L}=\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{L}H_{\ell}^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> as an <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">signal matrices</em></p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(X_{0},\ldots,X_{L})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(X_{0},\ldots,X_{L}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\ell+1)\times R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\ell+1)\times R</annotation></semantics></math> matrix of spherical harmonic coefficients <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\ell}=(x_{m}^{\ell}[r])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\ell}=(x_{m}^{\ell}[r])</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=-\ell,\ldots,\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=-\ell,\ldots,\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1,\ldots,R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1,\ldots,R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First moment.</span> The first non-uniform population moment is an element of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> which
is represented by the <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of matrices</p>
<table id="S2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(\rho,x)=(\hat{\rho}(H_{0})^{*}X_{0},\ldots,\hat{\rho}(H_{\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}L})^{*}X_{L})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;" mathcolor="#008000">L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(\rho,x)=(\hat{\rho}(H_{0})^{*}X_{0},\ldots,\hat{\rho}(H_{\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}L})^{*}X_{L}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, if we know the first moment, we know each of the matrices
<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In terms of the Wigner and spherical harmonic bases, the <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m^{\prime},s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m^{\prime},s)</annotation></semantics></math> entry of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}X_{\ell})_{(m^{\prime},s)}=\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell}\overline{\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]}\,x^{\ell}_{m}[s]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}X_{\ell})_{(m^{\prime},s)}=\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell}\overline{\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]}\,x^{\ell}_{m}[s].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Note that, because <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability distribution,
<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{G}\rho(g)\;dg=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{G}\rho(g)\;dg=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is always known from the first moment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second moment.</span>
The tensor <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes V</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes
as a sum of tensor products <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}^{R}\otimes H_{j}^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}^{R}\otimes H_{j}^{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In turn, we can view <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}^{R}\otimes H_{j}^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}^{R}\otimes H_{j}^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> as a sum of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> summands all equal to <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}\otimes H_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}\otimes H_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The summand
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}\otimes H_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}\otimes H_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes into irreducibles as</p>
<table id="S2.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H_{i}\otimes H_{j})=\bigoplus_{\ell=|i-j|}^{i+j}H_{{}_{\ell}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{i}\otimes H_{j})=\bigoplus_{\ell=|i-j|}^{i+j}H_{{}_{\ell}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, the tensor <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}\otimes X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}\otimes X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes
into components <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{i}\otimes X_{j})_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{i}\otimes X_{j})_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, which live in <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{\ell})^{R^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{\ell})^{R^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Viewed this way, the second moment of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, which is an element of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes V</annotation></semantics></math>, decomposes
as a sum</p>
<table id="S2.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(\rho,x)=\sum_{i,j}\left(\sum_{\ell=|i-j|}^{i+j}\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}(X_{i}\otimes X_{j})_{\ell}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(\rho,x)=\sum_{i,j}\left(\sum_{\ell=|i-j|}^{i+j}\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}(X_{i}\otimes X_{j})_{\ell}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where the inner sum reflects the decomposition of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}^{R}\otimes H_{j}^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}^{R}\otimes H_{j}^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> into its isotypical pieces following
the decomposition (<a href="#S2.E21" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). In particular, if we know the second moment we know each of
the summands <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}(X_{i}\otimes X_{j})_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}(X_{i}\otimes X_{j})_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> in the decomposition (<a href="#S2.E22" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>). We will denote
by <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(x;H_{i},H_{j},H_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(x;H_{i},H_{j},H_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> the summand <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{k})^{*}(X_{i}\otimes X_{j})_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{k})^{*}(X_{i}\otimes X_{j})_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The columns of the matrices <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the vectors <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}[s]\in H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}[s]\in H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1,\ldots,R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1,\ldots,R</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the tensor <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}\otimes X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}\otimes X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as a collection of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> tensors <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}[s_{1}]\otimes x^{j}[s_{2}]\in H_{i}\otimes H_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}[s_{1}]\otimes x^{j}[s_{2}]\in H_{i}\otimes H_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s_{1},s_{2}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s_{1},s_{2}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1.2. Clebsch-Gordan coefficients ‣ 2.1. Harmonic analysis on compact groups ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> to the second moment (<a href="#S2.E22" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>), we obtain an explicit formula in terms of Clebsch-Gordan coefficients for the action of the operator
<math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{i}[s_{1}]\otimes x^{j}[s_{2}])_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{i}[s_{1}]\otimes x^{j}[s_{2}])_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To make this precise, we change notation slightly and fix bands <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the triangle inequality and shell indices <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2},s_{3}\in\{1,\ldots,R\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2},s_{3}\in\{1,\ldots,R\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,
for <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\ell_{1}\leq m\leq\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\ell_{1}\leq m\leq\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry in the vector
<math id="S2.SS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell_{1}})^{*}(x^{\ell_{2}}[s_{2}]\otimes x^{\ell_{3}}[s_{3}])_{\ell_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell_{1}})^{*}(x^{\ell_{2}}[s_{2}]\otimes x^{\ell_{3}}[s_{3}])_{\ell_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(23)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{\ell_{1}}[m],H_{\ell_{2}}[s_{2}],H_{\ell_{3}}[s_{3}])\coloneq\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}k_{2}+k_{3}=k_{1}\\
|k_{i}|\leq\ell_{i}\end{subarray}}\langle\ell_{2}\,k_{2}\;\ell_{3}\,k_{3}\mid\ell_{1}\,k_{1}\rangle\,\overline{\hat{\rho}^{\ell_{1}}_{k_{1}}[m]}\,x^{\ell_{2}}_{k_{2}}[s_{2}]\,x^{\ell_{3}}_{k_{3}}[s_{3}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{\ell_{1}}[m],H_{\ell_{2}}[s_{2}],H_{\ell_{3}}[s_{3}])\coloneq\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}k_{2}+k_{3}=k_{1}\\
|k_{i}|\leq\ell_{i}\end{subarray}}\langle\ell_{2}\,k_{2}\;\ell_{3}\,k_{3}\mid\ell_{1}\,k_{1}\rangle\,\overline{\hat{\rho}^{\ell_{1}}_{k_{1}}[m]}\,x^{\ell_{2}}_{k_{2}}[s_{2}]\,x^{\ell_{3}}_{k_{3}}[s_{3}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Main results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin this section by explicitly introducing the signal and distribution models. These models will also serve us in the numerical experiments in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Then, we state the two main theorems on signal recovery from the second moment for a generic distribution on <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) and one for a generic in-plane uniform distribution on <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>). The results are then proved in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Proof of Theorem ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Proof of Theorem ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Main results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to present the main theoretical results of this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in L^{2}(\SO(3))</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution whose Fourier coefficients <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> are invertible
for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\ell\leq L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\ell\leq L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is a generic signal, then the pair <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho_{L},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho_{L},x)</annotation></semantics></math> is determined, up to a global rotation, by the first and second moments <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(\rho,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(\rho,x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(\rho,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(\rho,x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By generic signal we mean that
there is a non-empty Zariski open subset of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the conclusion of the theorem holds. Note that this set may depend on the distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> assumes that the Fourier coefficients of the distribution are invertible
(at least up to band <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>), this assumption may not be experimentally justified. In particular, in cryo‑EM the distribution of in‑plane rotations is uniform, since each in‑plane rotation is equally likely to occur <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. The following theorem shows that in this important scenario—where the in‑plane rotation distribution is uniform but otherwise generic—the theory continues to hold, with only a modest increase in the required number of shells.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> be an in-plane uniform distribution
whose non-vanishing Fourier coefficients <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\rho}_{m}^{\ell}[0]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\rho}_{m}^{\ell}[0]\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\ell\leq L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\ell\leq L</annotation></semantics></math>
are generic. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, then, for a generic signal
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the pair <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho_{L},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho_{L},x)</annotation></semantics></math> is determined, up to a global rotation, by the first and second moments <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}(\rho,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}(\rho,x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(\rho,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(\rho,x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By generic Fourier coefficients and generic signal we mean the following. If we let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of all possible
<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\rho}_{m}^{\ell}[0]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\rho}_{m}^{\ell}[0]\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\ell\leq L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\ell\leq L</annotation></semantics></math>, then there is a Zariski open set in <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following holds:
For every in-plane uniform distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> whose non-vanishing Fourier coefficients lie in this Zariski open set, there is a corresponding Zariski open set in the signal space <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in this open set the conclusion of the theorem holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the class of distributions and signals defined in Theorems <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
In the high-noise regime, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,\sigma\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,\sigma\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the sample complexity required to estimate a signal from observations of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) scales as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\omega(\sigma^{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\omega(\sigma^{4})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The optimality of the results)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is unclear whether the results of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> are optimal. In particular, the bounds of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=4</annotation></semantics></math> may be an artifact of the proof technique of the specific algorithm we analyze.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> is inductive and constructive. For each <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, we first use the second moment to solve for the generalized Fourier coefficient <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming that Fourier coefficients <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> and signal coefficients <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are known for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq l</annotation></semantics></math>. Having done this, we can easily determine the signal coefficient matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> from the first moment because the first moment yields <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})^{*}X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, if <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible we can determine <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> as well. This alternating procedure is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2. Proof of Theorem ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<div id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:345.0pt;">
          
<div id="S3.SS2.p2.p1" class="ltx_para"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="389.39" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 427.03 389.39" width="427.03"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,389.39) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(224.46,0) translate(0,339.26)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000080;--ltx-fill-color:#000080;--ltx-fg-color:#000080;" color="#000080" fill="#000080" stroke="#000080" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -107.72 35.91)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-fo-width:2.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.17em;font-size:11.5pt;" color="#000000" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="42.21"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000080;">Signal</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#994D00;--ltx-fill-color:#994D00;--ltx-fg-color:#994D00;" color="#994D00" fill="#994D00" stroke="#994D00" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 44.04 34.57)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;--ltx-fo-width:5.35em;--ltx-fo-height:0.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:11.5pt;" color="#000000" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="85.15"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#994D00;">Distribution</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B3B3B3;--ltx-fill-color:#ECECEC;" fill="#ECECEC" stroke="#B3B3B3" stroke-width="0.6pt"><path d="M -37.03 19.69 L -136.2 19.69 C -139.25 19.69 -141.73 17.21 -141.73 14.15 L -141.73 -14.15 C -141.73 -17.21 -139.25 -19.69 -136.2 -19.69 L -37.03 -19.69 C -33.97 -19.69 -31.5 -17.21 -31.5 -14.15 L -31.5 14.15 C -31.5 17.21 -33.97 19.69 -37.03 19.69 Z M -141.73 -19.69"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.6pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -95.4 -3.56)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.11em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="9.89" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="17.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B3B3B3;--ltx-fill-color:#ECECEC;" fill="#ECECEC" stroke="#B3B3B3" stroke-width="0.6pt"><path d="M 136.2 19.69 L 37.03 19.69 C 33.97 19.69 31.5 17.21 31.5 14.15 L 31.5 -14.15 C 31.5 -17.21 33.97 -19.69 37.03 -19.69 L 136.2 -19.69 C 139.25 -19.69 141.73 -17.21 141.73 -14.15 L 141.73 14.15 C 141.73 17.21 139.25 19.69 136.2 19.69 Z M 31.5 -19.69"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.6pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 69 -3.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.78em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.24em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="13.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.99)" width="35.24"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">H</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B3B3B3;--ltx-fill-color:#ECECEC;" fill="#ECECEC" stroke="#B3B3B3" stroke-width="0.6pt"><path d="M -37.03 -51.18 L -136.2 -51.18 C -139.25 -51.18 -141.73 -53.66 -141.73 -56.72 L -141.73 -85.02 C -141.73 -88.07 -139.25 -90.55 -136.2 -90.55 L -37.03 -90.55 C -33.97 -90.55 -31.5 -88.07 -31.5 -85.02 L -31.5 -56.72 C -31.5 -53.66 -33.97 -51.18 -37.03 -51.18 Z M -141.73 -90.55"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.6pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -95.4 -74.43)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.11em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="9.89" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.51)" width="17.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.p2.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#994D00;--ltx-fill-color:#FFF2E6;" fill="#FFF2E6" stroke="#994D00" stroke-width="0.6pt"><path d="M 136.2 -51.18 L 37.03 -51.18 C 33.97 -51.18 31.5 -53.66 31.5 -56.72 L 31.5 -85.02 C 31.5 -88.07 33.97 -90.55 37.03 -90.55 L 136.2 -90.55 C 139.25 -90.55 141.73 -88.07 141.73 -85.02 L 141.73 -56.72 C 141.73 -53.66 139.25 -51.18 136.2 -51.18 Z M 31.5 -90.55"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.6pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 69 -74.31)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.78em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.24em;font-size: