C(\{x^{\lfloor\ell/2\rfloor,\lceil\ell/2\rceil}_{s,t}[2,1]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{x^{\lfloor\ell/2\rfloor,\lceil\ell/2\rceil}_{s,t}[2,1]\})_{\ell}\\
\vdots&amp;&amp;\vdots\\
C(\{x^{\lfloor\ell/2\rfloor,\lceil\ell/2\rceil}_{s,t}[R,1]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{x^{\lfloor\ell/2\rfloor,\lceil\ell/2\rceil}_{s,t}[R,1]\})_{\ell}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since the forms <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\{x^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2},s_{3}]\})_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\{x^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2},s_{3}]\})_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=-\ell,\ldots,\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=-\ell,\ldots,\ell</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent for each fixed <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{1},s_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{1},s_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has full rank over the field of rational functions. Thus, for generic <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho,x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix has rank <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, the system has a unique solution for the entries of the Fourier coefficient matrix
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ Induction step. ‣ 3.2. Proof of Theorem ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> and with it Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> has a similar structure to the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. However, because the Fourier coefficients of an in-plane uniform distribution all have rank one, we cannot use the first moment to solve for the unknown matrices <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> if we already know the Fourier coefficient
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>. Because <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[m^{\prime}]=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the only non-zero terms in the second moment have the form</p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{i}[0],H_{j}[s_{2}],H_{k}[s_{3}])," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{i}[0],H_{j}[s_{2}],H_{k}[s_{3}]),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for varying values of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j,k\in[0,L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j,k\in[0,L]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2},s_{3}\in[1,R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2},s_{3}\in[1,R]</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Base case.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The base case is essentially the same as in the proof Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Because we have at least three shells, we may assume that the matrix <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank at least three and can therefore solve for the three non-zero entries in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> from the first moment <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{1})X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{1})X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Induction step.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose all terms of the distribution and signal have been determined up to band <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\rho}^{i}_{k}[0]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\rho}^{i}_{k}[0]\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{i}_{k}[s]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{i}_{k}[s]\}</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-i\leq k\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-i\leq k\leq i</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: The case <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Let <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{1})\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{1})\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times R}</annotation></semantics></math> be fixed. We first solve for the distribution term, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the equations <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M_{2}(H_{2}[0],H_{1}[s_{2}],H_{1}[s_{3}])\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{2}(H_{2}[0],H_{1}[s_{2}],H_{1}[s_{3}])\}</annotation></semantics></math> with indices taking values in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s_{2}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s_{2}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{3}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{3}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq s_{3}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq s_{3}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2R-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2R-1</annotation></semantics></math> equations, which is at least <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(H_{2})=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(H_{2})=5</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now we solve for the signal component <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For fixed <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s_{3}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s_{3}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> equations
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{1}[0],H_{1}[s_{2}],H_{2}[s_{3}])\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{1}[0],H_{1}[s_{2}],H_{2}[s_{3}])\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s_{2}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s_{2}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>
and the equation
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{2}[0],H_{1}[1],H_{2}[s_{3}])\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{2}[0],H_{1}[1],H_{2}[s_{3}])\}</annotation></semantics></math>
involve algebraically independent coefficients. Therefore, we
obtain <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R+1\geq\dim(H_{2})=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R+1\geq\dim(H_{2})=5</annotation></semantics></math> (when <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>) independent equations, which allows us to
solve for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}[s_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}[s_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: The case <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Fix <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s_{3}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s_{3}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>. We solve for the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math> unknowns <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{\ell}_{k}[s_{3}]\}_{k=-\ell}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{\ell}_{k}[s_{3}]\}_{k=-\ell}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> using invariants of the form <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(H_{i}[0],H_{\ell-i}[s_{2}],H_{\ell}[s_{3}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(H_{i}[0],H_{\ell-i}[s_{2}],H_{\ell}[s_{3}])</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s_{2}\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s_{2}\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For fixed <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the second moment equation (<a href="#S2.E23" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{i}[0],H_{\ell-i}[s_{2}],H_{\ell}[s_{3}])=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}k_{2}+k_{3}=k_{1}\\
|k_{1}|\leq i,|k_{2}|\leq(\ell-i),|k_{3}|\leq i\end{subarray}}\langle(\ell-i)\,k_{2}\;\ell\,k_{3}\mid i\,k_{1}\rangle\,\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}_{k_{1}}[0]}\,x^{\ell-i}_{k_{2}}[s_{2}]\,x^{\ell}_{k_{3}}[s_{3}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{i}[0],H_{\ell-i}[s_{2}],H_{\ell}[s_{3}])=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}k_{2}+k_{3}=k_{1}\\
|k_{1}|\leq i,|k_{2}|\leq(\ell-i),|k_{3}|\leq i\end{subarray}}\langle(\ell-i)\,k_{2}\;\ell\,k_{3}\mid i\,k_{1}\rangle\,\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}_{k_{1}}[0]}\,x^{\ell-i}_{k_{2}}[s_{2}]\,x^{\ell}_{k_{3}}[s_{3}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">gives a linear equation in the unknowns <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\ell}_{k}[s_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\ell}_{k}[s_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math>, with coefficients</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}[0]},x^{\ell-i}[s_{2}])_{k}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}k_{1}-k_{2}=k\end{subarray}}\langle(\ell-i)\,k_{1}\;\ell\,k\mid i\,k_{1}\rangle\,\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}_{k_{1}}[0]}x^{\ell-i}_{k_{2}}[s_{2}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}[0]},x^{\ell-i}[s_{2}])_{k}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}k_{1}-k_{2}=k\end{subarray}}\langle(\ell-i)\,k_{1}\;\ell\,k\mid i\,k_{1}\rangle\,\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}_{k_{1}}[0]}x^{\ell-i}_{k_{2}}[s_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">that are bilinear forms in the variables <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2}]\coloneq\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}_{s}[0]}\,x^{\ell-i}_{t}[s_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2}]\coloneq\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}_{s}[0]}\,x^{\ell-i}_{t}[s_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S[R]=\{(i,s_{2}):1\leq i\leq\ell-1,\,1\leq s_{2}\leq R\}\subset[1,\ell-1]\times[1,R]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S[R]=\{(i,s_{2}):1\leq i\leq\ell-1,\,1\leq s_{2}\leq R\}\subset[1,\ell-1]\times[1,R].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Taking <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,s_{2})\in S[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,s_{2})\in S[R]</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S[R]|=(\ell-1)\cdot R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S[R]|=(\ell-1)\cdot R</annotation></semantics></math> linear equations. For distinct pairs <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,s_{2}),(i^{\prime},s_{2}^{\prime})\in S[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,s_{2}),(i^{\prime},s_{2}^{\prime})\in S[R]</annotation></semantics></math>, there are no algebraic relations between the corresponding sets of monomials <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2}]\}_{s,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2}]\}_{s,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y^{i^{\prime},\ell-i^{\prime}}_{s,t}[s_{2}^{\prime}]\}_{s,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y^{i^{\prime},\ell-i^{\prime}}_{s,t}[s_{2}^{\prime}]\}_{s,t}</annotation></semantics></math>: if <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the variables lie in different bands, and if <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}\neq s_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}\neq s_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the variables lie in different shells. Thus, we may treat them as distinct sets of variables.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Using the shorthand <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\{y^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2}]\})_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\{y^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2}]\})_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for the coefficient
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}[0]},x^{\ell-i}[s_{2}])_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\overline{\hat{\rho}^{i}[0]},x^{\ell-i}[s_{2}])_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
the coefficient matrix is</p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(25)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\begin{pmatrix}C(\{y^{1,\ell-1}_{s,t}[1]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{y^{1,\ell-1}_{s,t}[1]\})_{\ell}\\
\vdots&amp;&amp;\vdots\\
C(\{y^{1,\ell-1}_{s,t}[R]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{y^{1,L-1}_{s,t}[R]\})_{\ell}\\
C(\{y^{2,L-2}_{s,t}[1]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{y^{2,L-2}_{s,t}[1]\})_{\ell}\\
\vdots&amp;&amp;\vdots\\
C(\{y^{L-1,1}_{s,t}[R]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{y^{L-1,1}_{s,t}[R]\})_{\ell}\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\begin{pmatrix}C(\{y^{1,\ell-1}_{s,t}[1]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{y^{1,\ell-1}_{s,t}[1]\})_{\ell}\\
\vdots&amp;&amp;\vdots\\
C(\{y^{1,\ell-1}_{s,t}[R]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{y^{1,L-1}_{s,t}[R]\})_{\ell}\\
C(\{y^{2,L-2}_{s,t}[1]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{y^{2,L-2}_{s,t}[1]\})_{\ell}\\
\vdots&amp;&amp;\vdots\\
C(\{y^{L-1,1}_{s,t}[R]\})_{-\ell}&amp;\cdots&amp;C(\{y^{L-1,1}_{s,t}[R]\})_{\ell}\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Since the forms <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\{y^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2}]\})_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\{y^{i,\ell-i}_{s,t}[s_{2}]\})_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=-\ell,\ldots,\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=-\ell,\ldots,\ell</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent for each fixed <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,s_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,s_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has full rank over the field of rational functions. Thus, for generic <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho,x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix has rank <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\left(\ell-1)\cdot R,2\ell+1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\left(\ell-1)\cdot R,2\ell+1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The system has a unique solution when <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell-1)\cdot R\geq 2\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell-1)\cdot R\geq 2\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=4</annotation></semantics></math>, this gives <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4(\ell-1)=4\ell-4\geq 2\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4(\ell-1)=4\ell-4\geq 2\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which holds when <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recovery of the distribution band <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ Induction step. ‣ 3.2. Proof of Theorem ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, if the signal coefficients are generic, then the distribution
coefficients <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}^{\ell}_{m}[0]</annotation></semantics></math> are determined from the second moment invariants,
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{\ell}[0],H_{i}[s_{2}],H_{\ell-i}[s_{3}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}(\rho,x;H_{\ell}[0],H_{i}[s_{2}],H_{\ell-i}[s_{3}])</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq\lfloor\ell/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq\lfloor\ell/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, provided that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, for a generic distribution and signal <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\rho,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\rho,x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can recover <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> up to the action of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> from the second moment.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Algorithm and Numerical experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first explicitly introduce the algorithm derived from the proofs in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Then, we empirically validate the theoretical results and demonstrate that our frequency marching algorithm, based on the second moment, successfully recovers the signal when the distribution is non-uniform. Furthermore, we compare its performance against the third-moment method proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, analyzing the robustness of both estimators with respect to noise, sample complexity, and the degree of nonuniformity in the data.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Signal model</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the numerical part, we model the signal as</p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(26)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(r,\theta,\varphi)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{L}\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell}\sum_{s=1}^{R}x_{m}^{\ell}[s]\,Y_{\ell}^{m}(\theta,\varphi)\,j_{\ell,s}(r)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>R</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(r,\theta,\varphi)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{L}\sum_{m=-\ell}^{\ell}\sum_{s=1}^{R}x_{m}^{\ell}[s]\,Y_{\ell}^{m}(\theta,\varphi)\,j_{\ell,s}(r).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This model is similar to (<a href="#S2.E14" title="In 2.2. Signal and distribution models ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), but the function is not discretized in the radial direction. Specifically, the radial direction is described by the normalized spherical Bessel functions <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\ell,s}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ell,s}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> defined as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\ell,s}(r)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{\lvert j_{\ell+1}(u_{\ell,s})\rvert}\,j_{\ell}(u_{\ell,s}r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ell,s}(r)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{\lvert j_{\ell+1}(u_{\ell,s})\rvert}\,j_{\ell}(u_{\ell,s}r)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is the spherical Bessel function of the first kind of order <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\ell,s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\ell,s}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes its <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>-th positive zero. The number of radial components is limited to a fixed number of shells <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>,
and such a function can also be represented as an element in the finite-dimensional <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>-representation <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{L}=\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{L}H_{\ell}^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{L}=\bigoplus_{\ell=0}^{L}H_{\ell}^{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. This representation is chosen for its superior numerical stability compared to the shell model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and the theoretical results established above follow directly in this setting.
The representation of the distribution is as in (<a href="#S2.E16" title="In 2.2. Signal and distribution models ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Algorithm</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The algorithm for signal recovery from the second moment is induced by our proofs in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, with slight changes that are explained below.
It is based on a frequency marching mechanism, where the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th frequency of the signal and the distribution is recovered linearly based on the knowledge of lower frequencies. A schematic illustration of this alternating procedure is given in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2. Proof of Theorem ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
We now formulate the algorithm explicitly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The algorithm begins by estimating the population moments from the empirical moments. While the first moment is unbiased and can therefore be estimated directly, the second moment includes a bias term. However, this bias affects only a small portion of the second moment, specifically, the component that is invariant under <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{y_{i}=g_{i}x+\varepsilon_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\subset(L^{2}(S^{2})_{L})^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{i}=g_{i}x+\varepsilon_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\subset(L^{2}(S^{2})_{L})^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> noisy, randomly rotated copies of the signal <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{\ell}[s]\in H_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{\ell}[s]\in H_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the components of the sample in the s-th radial shell of the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th band. For <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i}^{\ell_{2}}[s_{2}]\otimes y_{i}^{\ell_{3}}[s_{3}])_{\ell_{1}}\;\xrightarrow{n\to\infty}\;\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell_{1}})^{*}(x^{\ell_{2}}[s_{2}]\otimes x^{\ell_{3}}[s_{3}])_{\ell_{1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i}^{\ell_{2}}[s_{2}]\otimes y_{i}^{\ell_{3}}[s_{3}])_{\ell_{1}}\;\xrightarrow{n\to\infty}\;\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell_{1}})^{*}(x^{\ell_{2}}[s_{2}]\otimes x^{\ell_{3}}[s_{3}])_{\ell_{1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i}^{\ell}[s]\otimes y_{i}^{\ell}[s])_{0}\;\xrightarrow{n\to\infty}\;x^{\ell}[s]x^{\ell}[s]^{*}+\sigma^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i}^{\ell}[s]\otimes y_{i}^{\ell}[s])_{0}\;\xrightarrow{n\to\infty}\;x^{\ell}[s]x^{\ell}[s]^{*}+\sigma^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The noise <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contributes <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}I_{2\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}I_{2\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math> to diagonal blocks <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\ell}\otimes H_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\ell}\otimes H_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> of the second moment. Under the decomposition <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\ell}\otimes H_{\ell}=\bigoplus_{k=0}^{2\ell}H_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\ell}\otimes H_{\ell}=\bigoplus_{k=0}^{2\ell}H_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{2\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{2\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math> lies entirely in the trivial summand <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Projection onto <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> therefore annihilates the bias.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, the empirical second moment <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}y_{i}\otimes y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}y_{i}\otimes y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is biased by <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}I</annotation></semantics></math> due to the noise, and we therefore debias the estimator as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(27)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{2}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}y_{i}\otimes y_{i}-\sigma^{2}I." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{2}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}y_{i}\otimes y_{i}-\sigma^{2}I.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The empirical first moment is simply given by</p>
<table id="S4.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(28)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{1}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}y_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{1}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}y_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we aim to recover the signal and distribution coefficients. First, the signal coefficients <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{0}[s]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{0}[s]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s\leq R</annotation></semantics></math>, are recovered directly from the <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=0</annotation></semantics></math> component of the first moment (<a href="#S2.E19" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), since <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{0})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{0})=1</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(29)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{0}^{0}[s]={(\hat{M}_{1})_{0}}_{(0,s)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{0}^{0}[s]={(\hat{M}_{1})_{0}}_{(0,s)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The coefficients <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{m}^{1}[s],1\leq s\leq R,m\in\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{m}^{1}[s],1\leq s\leq R,m\in\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, are determined up to <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Orth(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Orth(3)</annotation></semantics></math> from the Gram matrix whose <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,s^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,s^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> entry is</p>
<table id="S4.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(30)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m=-1}^{1}x_{m}^{1}[s]\,\left({x_{m}^{1}}[s^{\prime}]\right)^{*}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m=-1}^{1}x_{m}^{1}[s]\,\left({x_{m}^{1}}[s^{\prime}]\right)^{*},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">which is obtained from the uniform second moment; see the base case of the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. The coefficients are recovered up to a global rotation and reflection by computing a matrix square root, e.g., via eigendecomposition. We can eliminate the reflection by utilizing one additional equation from the second moment. Then, from the second moment equations (<a href="#S2.E23" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>), we proceed:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the signal coefficients <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed, (<a href="#S2.E23" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) is linear in the distribution coefficients <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the distribution coefficients <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed, (<a href="#S2.E23" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) is linear in the signal coefficients of the highest band (when solving progressively).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.4" class="ltx_p">We note that although the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Main results ‣ 3. Main results ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> uses the first moment to recover signal coefficients, in practice we solve for both signal and distribution coefficients directly from the second moment equations (<a href="#S2.E23" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>), which subsumes the first moment information and yields better numerical performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distribution recovery.</span> Assuming signal coefficients for bands <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,\dots,\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,\dots,\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math> are known, we recover the distribution Fourier coefficients at band <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> by solving <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\ell+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\ell+1)</annotation></semantics></math> independent linear systems, one for each column <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}\in\{-\ell,\dots,\ell\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}\in\{-\ell,\dots,\ell\}</annotation></semantics></math> of the distribution Fourier coefficient matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>. For each column index <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, equation (<a href="#S2.E23" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) yields a linear system:</p>
<table id="S4.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(31)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}(x_{0\dots\ell-1})\,\overline{\hat{\rho}_{\ell}[s_{1}]}=b_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}(\hat{M}_{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}(x_{0\dots\ell-1})\,\overline{\hat{\rho}_{\ell}[s_{1}]}=b_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}(\hat{M}_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}_{\ell}[s_{1}]\in\mathbb{C}^{2\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}_{\ell}[s_{1}]\in\mathbb{C}^{2\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}(H_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>. The matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\ell+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\ell+1)</annotation></semantics></math> columns indexed by <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}\in\{-\ell,\ldots,\ell\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}\in\{-\ell,\ldots,\ell\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and rows indexed by quadruples <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{2},s_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{2},s_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\ell-\ell_{2}|\leq\ell_{3}\leq\min(\ell+\ell_{2},\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\ell-\ell_{2}|\leq\ell_{3}\leq\min(\ell+\ell_{2},\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2},s_{3}\in\{1,\ldots,R\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2},s_{3}\in\{1,\ldots,R\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each row has entries:</p>
<table id="S4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(32)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}}_{(\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{2},s_{3}),m_{1}}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}m_{2}+m_{3}=m_{1}\\
|m_{i}|\leq\ell_{i}\end{subarray}}\,\langle\ell_{2}\,m_{2}\,\ell_{3}\,m_{3}|\ell\,m_{1}\,\rangle\,x^{\ell_{2}}_{m_{2}}[s_{2}]\,x^{\ell_{3}}_{m_{3}}[s_{3}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}}_{(\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{2},s_{3}),m_{1}}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}m_{2}+m_{3}=m_{1}\\
|m_{i}|\leq\ell_{i}\end{subarray}}\,\langle\ell_{2}\,m_{2}\,\ell_{3}\,m_{3}|\ell\,m_{1}\,\rangle\,x^{\ell_{2}}_{m_{2}}[s_{2}]\,x^{\ell_{3}}_{m_{3}}[s_{3}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}\in\{-\ell,\ldots,\ell\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}\in\{-\ell,\ldots,\ell\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\ell_{2}}_{m_{2}}[s_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\ell_{2}}_{m_{2}}[s_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\ell_{3}}_{m_{3}}[s_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\ell_{3}}_{m_{3}}[s_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math> denote known signal coefficients from lower bands. The right-hand side vector is the empirical second moment components for bands <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell,\ell_{2},\ell_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell,\ell_{2},\ell_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and shells <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(33)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}]_{(\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{2},s_{3})}=[\hat{M}_{2}]_{\ell,\ell_{2},\ell_{3}}(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}]_{(\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{2},s_{3})}=[\hat{M}_{2}]_{\ell,\ell_{2},\ell_{3}}(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Signal recovery.</span> Conversely, if the distribution Fourier coefficients are known up to band <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and the signal coefficients are known up to band <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we recover the signal coefficients at band <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> by solving <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> independent linear systems, one for each shell <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\{1,\dots,R\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\{1,\dots,R\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each shell <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, equation (<a href="#S2.E23" title="In 2.3. A Fourier-theoretic perspective of SO(3) moments ‣ 2. Background ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) yields:</p>
<table id="S4.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(34)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{x}^{(s)}(\hat{\rho}_{0\dots\ell},x_{0\dots\ell-1})\,x_{\ell}[s]=b_{x}^{(s)}(\hat{M}_{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{x}^{(s)}(\hat{\rho}_{0\dots\ell},x_{0\dots\ell-1})\,x_{\ell}[s]=b_{x}^{(s)}(\hat{M}_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\ell}[s]=(x^{\ell}_{-\ell}[s],\ldots,x^{\ell}_{\ell}[s])^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\ell}[s]=(x^{\ell}_{-\ell}[s],\ldots,x^{\ell}_{\ell}[s])^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of the signal matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. The matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{x}^{(s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{x}^{(s)}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\ell+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\ell+1)</annotation></semantics></math> columns indexed by <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{-\ell,\ldots,\ell\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{-\ell,\ldots,\ell\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and rows indexed by sextuples <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following constraints: <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\ell_{1}&lt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\ell_{1}&lt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\ell_{1}-\ell_{2}|\leq\ell_{3}\leq\min(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\ell_{1}-\ell_{2}|\leq\ell_{3}\leq\min(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2},L)</annotation></semantics></math> (triangle inequality); exactly one of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\ell_{2},\ell_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\ell_{2},\ell_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> while the other is less than <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}\in\{-\ell_{1},\ldots,\ell_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}\in\{-\ell_{1},\ldots,\ell_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2},s_{3}\in\{1,\ldots,R\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2},s_{3}\in\{1,\ldots,R\}</annotation></semantics></math>; and crucially, the shell index corresponding to band <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}=s</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}=\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}=\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{3}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{3}=s</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{3}=\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{3}=\ell</annotation></semantics></math>). Without loss of generality, assume <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}=\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}=\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}=s</annotation></semantics></math>. Then each row has entries:</p>
<table id="S4.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(35)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{A_{x}^{(s)}}_{(\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}),m_{2}}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}m_{1}+m_{3}=m_{2}\\
|m_{i}|\leq\ell_{i}\end{subarray}}\,\langle\ell_{1}\,m_{1}\,\ell_{3}\,m_{3}|\ell\,m_{2}\,\rangle\,\overline{\hat{\rho}^{\ell_{1}}_{m_{1}}[s_{1}]}\,x^{\ell_{3}}_{m_{3}}[s_{3}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{A_{x}^{(s)}}_{(\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}),m_{2}}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}m_{1}+m_{3}=m_{2}\\
|m_{i}|\leq\ell_{i}\end{subarray}}\,\langle\ell_{1}\,m_{1}\,\ell_{3}\,m_{3}|\ell\,m_{2}\,\rangle\,\overline{\hat{\rho}^{\ell_{1}}_{m_{1}}[s_{1}]}\,x^{\ell_{3}}_{m_{3}}[s_{3}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{2}\in\{-\ell,\ldots,\ell\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{2}\in\{-\ell,\ldots,\ell\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}^{\ell_{1}}_{m_{1}}[s_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}^{\ell_{1}}_{m_{1}}[s_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> denote known distribution Fourier coefficients and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\ell_{3}}_{m_{3}}[s_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\ell_{3}}_{m_{3}}[s_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math> denote known signal coefficients from the lower band <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{3}&lt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{3}&lt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. The case where <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{3}=\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{3}=\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{3}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{3}=s</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}&lt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}&lt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math>) is constructed analogously. The right-hand side vector consists of the empirical second moment components:</p>
<table id="S4.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(36)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[b_{x}^{(s)}]_{(\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})}=[\hat{M}_{2}]_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3}}(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[b_{x}^{(s)}]_{(\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3},s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})}=[\hat{M}_{2}]_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3}}(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A pseudo-code of the algorithm is described in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Joint recovery of volume and distribution from the second moment
</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inputs:</span> Noisy observations <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, noise variance <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, number of shells <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, maximal frequency <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Outputs:</span> Signal <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and distribution <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> up to <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
            
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
            Compute and debias the second moment according to (<a href="#S4.E27" title="In 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
            Initialize <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\rho}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\rho}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
            Estimate <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{0}[s],1\leq s\leq R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{0}[s],1\leq s\leq R</annotation></semantics></math> from the first moment according to (<a href="#S4.E29" title="In 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
            Estimate <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{m}^{1}[s],1\leq s\leq R,m\in\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{m}^{1}[s],1\leq s\leq R,m\in\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> by applying matrix decomposition to the Gram matrix defined in (<a href="#S4.E30" title="In 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=1:L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=1:L</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}=1:2\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}=1:2\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
               Solve the system of equations (<a href="#S4.E31" title="In 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>): <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\hat{\rho}}_{\ell}[s_{1}]\leftarrow\arg\min_{\rho}\|A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}\hat{\rho}-b_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\hat{\rho}}_{\ell}[s_{1}]\leftarrow\arg\min_{\rho}\|A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}\hat{\rho}-b_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in (<a href="#S4.E32" title="In 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) and <math id="alg1.l10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\rho}^{(s_{1})}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in (<a href="#S4.E33" title="In 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
               <span id="alg1.l13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1\to R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1\to R</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
                 Solve the system of equations (<a href="#S4.E34" title="In 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>): <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{\ell}[s]\leftarrow\arg\min_{x}\|A_{x}^{(s)}x-b_{x}^{(s)}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{\ell}[s]\leftarrow\arg\min_{x}\|A_{x}^{(s)}x-b_{x}^{(s)}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="alg1.l14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{x}^{(s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{x}^{(s)}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in (<a href="#S4.E35" title="In 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) and <math id="alg1.l14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{x}^{(s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{x}^{(s)}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in (<a href="#S4.E36" title="In 4.2. Algorithm ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
               <span id="alg1.l15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              <span id="alg1.l16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
            <span id="alg1.l18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,\rho</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Experimental setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generated synthetic data using two volumes: the Plasmodium falciparum 80S ribosome <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> available from the Electron Microscopy Data Bank (EMDB)<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">
                    ∗
                  ∗\ast</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">
                    ∗
                  ∗\ast</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math></span>
              
              
            <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">https://www.ebi.ac.uk/emdb/</span></span></span></span> under accession code <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">EMD-2660</span>, and the TRPV1 structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, available as <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">EMD-8117</span>. The volumes were downsampled to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>31</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> voxels, and expanded into the truncated 3-D spherical-Bessel expansion of (<a href="#S4.E26" title="In 4.1. Signal model ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) up to degree <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> radial shells; <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> vary between the experiments.
All experiments were performed on a Linux server equipped with an Intel Xeon Gold 6252 CPU @ 2.10GHz and 1.5 TB of RAM. The algorithms were implemented in MATLAB. The code used to reproduce all numerical experiments is publicly available at</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">https://github.com/krshay/orbit-recovery-so3</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our observations follow (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>); the observations are generated in the coefficients domain. Unless stated otherwise, we simulate non-uniform viewing distributions by sampling the Euler angles <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> independently from a zero-mean Gaussian distribution with a standard deviation of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. The signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) is defined by</p>
<table id="S4.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(37)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SNR}=\frac{\|x\|_{\text{F}}}{\|\varepsilon\|_{\text{F}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>SNR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext>F</mtext></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext>F</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SNR}=\frac{\|x\|_{\text{F}}}{\|\varepsilon\|_{\text{F}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the expansion coefficients, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> the additive noise in coefficient space, and <span id="S4.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\text{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>F</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\text{F}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> the Frobenius norm. The recovery error is computed as</p>
<table id="S4.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(38)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Relative Error}=\frac{\|x-\hat{x}\|_{\text{F}}}{\|x\|_{\text{F}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Relative Error</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext>F</mtext></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext>F</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Relative Error}=\frac{\|x-\hat{x}\|_{\text{F}}}{\|x\|_{\text{F}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ground-truth expansion coefficients and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math> the recovered coefficients. We note that the global rotation ambiguity is resolved in our implementation by fixing the coefficients for <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=1</annotation></semantics></math> to their true values, rendering a search over <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> unnecessary.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Recovery from a set of noisy measurements</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by demonstrating a successful volume reconstruction from a set of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=50{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=50{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> noisy measurements with <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SNR}=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SNR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SNR}=1/2</annotation></semantics></math>; see Figures <a href="#S4.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 4.4. Recovery from a set of noisy measurements ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 4.4. Recovery from a set of noisy measurements ‣ 4. Algorithm and Numerical experiments ‣ Provable orbit recovery over SO(3) from the non-uniform second moment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>. These results use <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=13</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=8</annotation></semantics></math>. The molecular visualizations were produced using UCSF Chimera <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. The code ran for approximately <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> hours. The reconstruction error was <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> for the Plasmodium falciparum 80S ribosome, and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21\%</annotation></semantics></math> for the TRPV1 volume.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20590v1/80S_est_gray_noisy_measurement_yellow_est_green_true.png" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/266;" width="476" height="266" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Plasmodium falciparum 80S ribosome</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20590v1/TRPV1_est_gray_noisy_measurement_yellow_est_green_true.png" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/265;" width="476" height="265" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TRPV1 volume</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Volume reconstruction results from <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=50{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=50{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> noisy measurements with <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SNR}=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>SNR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SNR}=1/2</annotation></semantics></math>, using expansion parameters <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=13</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=8</annotation></semantics></math> shells. (a) Plasmodium falciparum 80S ribosome reconstruction. (b) TRPV1 volume reconstruction. In both panels, from left to right: a representative noisy measurement; the reconstructed volume; and the ground truth volume expanded to <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Condition numbers for signal and distribution bands for the reconstruction of the Plasmodium falciparum 80S ribosome.</span></figcaption>
